WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.659
INTRO:
Usually, the richer someone gets the more

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.230
conservative they are with their investments.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.200
This makes sense as the focus generally shifts
from wealth growth to wealth preservation

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.830
with growing riches.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.160
Even some of the richest people in the world
who took massive risks to get to where they

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.260
are have grown more conservative with time.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.449
Bill Gates, for example, owned 45% of Microsoft
when they went public in 1986.

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:27.980
But since then, he’s basically sold all
his as he owns just 1.3% of Microsoft today.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:34.070
Now, Bill is still worth $128 billion, so
I think it worked out alright.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:38.490
But if he had just held onto his initial stake,
he’d be worth over a trillion dollars.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:43.070
Similarly, Warren Buffett was famous for beating
the S&amp;P 500 for decades.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.550
But today, he’s holding $150 billion worth
of cash.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:52.010
So, clearly, even the richest people in the
world have gotten more conservative with time.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.540
One billionaire who never toned it down though
was Bill Hwang.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.280
With time, he placed riskier and risker bets
which initially paid off growing his fortune

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.810
hundreds of fold.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.580
But like with all gamblers, when you bet it
all, it just takes one bad bet to wipe you

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.580
out.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.619
And that’s exactly what happened last year
when Bill lost $20 billion in 2 days.

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:20.340
So, here’s the story of Bill Hwang and how
he made and lost billions in record time.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.630
BILL HWANG:
Taking a look back, Bill’s story circles

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.890
back to South Korea to the year 1964.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.719
Like many immigrant stories, Bill’s origins
were rather grim.

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:36.450
Bill says that his parents struggled to make
ends meet and that he had to pick up many

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.700
part-time jobs to help them out.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.240
Despite their bleak situation, the family
never lost hope.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.710
They were strong Christians and believed that
one way or another, things would eventually

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.999
work out.

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:51.890
As Bill reached his teenage years, it seemed
like their wishes were coming true as things

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.530
were finally starting to get better.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:59.340
The family was able to immigrate to Las Vegas
and Bill’s father got a job as a pastor.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.700
Unfortunately, Bill basically knew no English
which made it extremely difficult for him

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.130
to make friends, excel in school, or get a
job.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:11.670
But with persistence, Bill was able to score
a nightshift at a Mcdonald’s, and Bill says

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.320
that this is where he learned to speak English.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.890
Just as things were starting to pick up though,
Bill’s father died.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.970
This shattered the family’s morale and they
would relocate to Los Angeles.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:26.430
Despite the hardships, Bill knew that with
his father gone, he was truly on his own.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.440
So, he put his head down and got to work.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.780
Bill eventually secured a spot at UCLA and
majored in economics, and after his bachelor’s,

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.280
Bill turned around and got an MBA from Carnegie
Mellon University.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.880
Despite this strong educational background,
Bill struggled to score a high paying job

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.140
out of college.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.270
And he ended up working as a low-level salesman
and Hyundai securities.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:52.000
Note that this was not at Hyundai motors,
so Bill was not a car salesman.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.320
Rather he was a securities broker or basically
a stock salesman.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.350
These guys aren’t nearly as common today
due to the rise of online brokerages that

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.500
are intuitive and simple to use.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.280
But back in the day, securities brokers would
call up a bunch of people and try to convince

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.740
them to buy a particular stock or bond.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.870
This job provided Bill with a comfortable
life, but given that he was still working

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.510
here when was 33 years old, it seemed like
Bill would never achieve much more than mediocrity

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.030
career-wise.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.860
That was until he caught the eye of Julian
Robertson.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.160
Julian was the founder of the hedge fund Tiger
Management Corporation.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.060
By this point, Tiger Management was already
managing billions, and they were one of the

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.610
fastest-growing hedge funds in the world.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:36.040
But like Bill, Julian had humble roots and
was initially a stockbroker himself.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.460
Julian felt that he could see his younger
self within Bill, so he offered Bill a golden

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.810
ticket.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.020
TIGER CUB:
Julian offered Bill a job as a financial analyst

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:50.500
at Tiger Management, and Bill of course took
the job.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.150
Bill didn’t just want to be another financial
analyst though, he wanted to offer something

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.540
unique to the fund.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.820
So, he proposed the idea of expanding Tiger
Management into Asian investments.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:04.000
While Bill had assimilated quite well into
American culture, he never let go of his Asian

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.000
culture either.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.510
Bill felt that he had a unique perspective
on Asian companies and that he was the guy

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.120
for Asian investments.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.290
Julian gave him a shot, and it quickly became
clear that Bill wasn’t bluffing.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.959
He hit home run after home run investing in
dot-com favorites like SK Telecom.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.419
Amongst his peers, Bill became known as “the
Michael Jordan of Asian Investing”.

00:04:23.419 --> 00:04:29.100
With the help of Bill, Tiger Management over
doubled their assets between 1997 and 1998

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.449
from $10.5 billion to $22 billion.

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:36.370
This made Tiger Management the second-largest
hedge fund in the world.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.810
But it wasn’t all sunshines and rainbows.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:42.759
To achieve these mad returns, Bill was investing
in some of the most speculative names of the

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:44.080
Dotcom bubble.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.020
Many of his picks would crash 80 to 90% in
the early 2000s and they haven’t moved since.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.229
But, fortunately for Bill, he would never
have to experience this downfall as Julian

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:54.699
would soon shut down Tiger management.

00:04:54.699 --> 00:05:00.080
You see, Tiger had made an investment in US
Airways that wasn’t doing so well.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.909
Aside from this, Julian was already in his
60s, he was already a multi-billionaire, and

00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:09.590
he could sense the stock market was getting
superheated.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.270
So, he decided that he didn’t want to push
his luck.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:18.139
In March of 2000, Julian went ahead and sold
most of his stocks, shut down Tiger Management,

00:05:18.139 --> 00:05:21.330
and distributed all remaining profits to investors.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.979
Considering that March 24, 2000, was literally
the peak of the Dotcom bubble, you could say

00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:27.710
this was spectacular timing.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.409
Or maybe, Julian’s selling was the catalyst
that turned the tide.

00:05:31.409 --> 00:05:32.419
Who knows?

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:35.939
But either way, shutting down Tiger Management
was a great call for Julian.

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:41.159
Now, we should note that Julian ended bag
holding US Airways to 0 as the company ended

00:05:41.159 --> 00:05:42.490
up going bankrupt.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:47.159
Nonetheless, by shutting down Tiger in 2000,
Julian significantly reduced his losses and

00:05:47.159 --> 00:05:48.270
made it out quite well.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.630
Too bad, these skills didn’t rub off on
Bill.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.689
Anyway, while Julian was done with running
his own hedge fund, he wasn’t completely

00:05:55.689 --> 00:05:57.999
done with the hedge fund space quite yet.

00:05:57.999 --> 00:06:03.180
He was well aware that by shutting down Tiger,
he put 97 bright investors out of a job.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.819
So, Julian launched a seed funding program
that gave his former employees capital to

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:08.309
start their own hedge funds.

00:06:08.309 --> 00:06:12.600
By the end of the decade, Julian helped launch
38 different hedge funds which are often referred

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.669
to as Tiger Cubs.

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:18.289
As you would guess, Bill used this opportunity
to launch his own hedge fund called Tiger

00:06:18.289 --> 00:06:20.479
Asia.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:25.229
BANNED:
From the very beginning, Bill ran Tiger Asia

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:26.469
on the edge.

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:31.840
Most of his positions were outsized leveraged
bets that could go really well or really bad.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.110
For the first couple of years, Tiger Asia
posted stellar numbers as the market recovered

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.669
from the dot-com crash.

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:44.309
By 2007, Bill boasted a nutty 40% annualized
return managing a total of $8 billion.

00:06:44.309 --> 00:06:48.360
But, when you’re constantly playing with
fire, it’s only a matter of time until you

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.599
get burned.

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.430
And Bill’s first burn came in late 2008
during the height of the great recession.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:58.159
Considering the timing, you would think that
Bill got burned by an outsized bullish bet

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:01.029
which would actually be quite understandable.

00:07:01.029 --> 00:07:05.059
But ironically, Bill actually got destroyed
by a short position.

00:07:05.059 --> 00:07:09.780
If you’re not familiar with shorting, it’s
basically the opposite of regular stock investing.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.129
You make money when the stock goes down and
you lose money when the stock goes up.

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:19.039
Anyway, considering the recession, Bill thought
that it would be a great idea to short Volkswagen.

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:23.120
In theory, he should’ve been right, but
Porsche’s rapidly growing stake in Volkswagen

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.210
set off an insane short squeeze.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:29.319
And if you thought the Gamestop short squeeze
was nuts, this was on another level.

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:33.939
The percentage gains weren’t as much as
Gamestop as Volkswagen only grew 4 to 5x from

00:07:33.939 --> 00:07:35.960
$200 to nearly $1000.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:39.680
But given that Volkswagen wasn’t initially
a penny stock, the short squeeze actually

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.169
made Volkswagen the biggest company in the
world momentarily.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:48.180
This caused Tiger Asia to lose 23% of their
entire portfolio, and as you would guess,

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.869
investors weren’t happy.

00:07:49.869 --> 00:07:53.210
They were furious that Bill was even involved
with Volkswagen.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:58.560
The hedge fund was called Tiger Asia and it
was supposed to be focused on Asian investments.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.990
Many investors pulled their money out of Tiger
Asia after the fiasco, but Bill did recover.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.879
His colleagues say that he learned from this
mistake and that ever since this experience,

00:08:07.879 --> 00:08:12.470
he opted to buy into heavily shorted stocks
as opposed to just shorting them as well.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:17.000
Over the next couple of years, Bill recovered
from his mistake, but unfortunately, his next

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.729
mistake would be unsalvageable.

00:08:18.729 --> 00:08:23.639
In 2012, Tiger Asia would get convicted of
insider trading.

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:28.669
Bill was fined $44 million and he was permanently
banned from managing other people’s money.

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:32.580
With Bill out of the hedge fund scene, you
would think that this was the end of Bill’s

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:33.580
journey.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.380
But this was just the beginning of Archegos.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.419
ARCHEGOS:
Since Bill could no longer manage outside

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.919
money, he decided to manage his own fund.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.910
He had already made hundreds of millions during
his time in Wall Street, so he didn’t need

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.259
outside money.

00:08:49.259 --> 00:08:53.980
If Bill just went ahead and invested his own
money, it wouldn’t have been that bad, but

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.310
he wasn’t happy with just making ordinary
returns.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:02.149
Bill wanted extraordinary returns, so he turned
to his favorite strategy: leveraged trading.

00:09:02.149 --> 00:09:05.769
Bill went ahead and borrowed money from banks
in order to fund his trading.

00:09:05.769 --> 00:09:10.450
Now, this itself is extremely dangerous, but
Bill would take it to the next level.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:14.959
You see, banks aren’t stupid enough to loan
Bill hundreds of millions and eventually billions

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:17.030
to go gamble in the stock market.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:20.880
Banks want to ensure that there’s very little
risk of losing their own capital, so they

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.970
use collateral and margin calls.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:27.690
For example, let’s say that you wanted to
buy $200 worth of Apple shares but you only

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.440
have $100.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.940
Chase will happily loan you the other $100
if you put up your $100 worth of Apple shares

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.370
as collateral.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.649
This way, they have quite a bit of leeway.

00:09:37.649 --> 00:09:42.520
Apple stock would have to crash 50% for the
total position to be worth less than $100,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:47.590
and the second that Apple does reach -50%,
Chase will sell your stock and get their money

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:48.590
back.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:52.110
On the other hand, if the stock skyrockets,
they’re happy to just collect interest and

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.810
go on with their day.

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:57.960
But what if you use the same $100 as collateral
with many different lenders.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:02.870
Now, as a retail investor, banks will make
sure you don’t do this as they don’t really

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:03.870
trust you.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:07.089
But with someone like Bill, they were much
looser with their restrictions which allowed

00:10:07.089 --> 00:10:11.290
Bill to take on 3 to 1, 5 to 1, and even 8
to 1 leverage.

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:17.190
Bill used this strategy to 100x his portfolio
from $200 million to $20 billion, and no one

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:18.550
noticed a thing.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:22.370
You see, as long as your positions are playing
out, there’s no problem with leverage.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.800
It’s only when your positions go south that
you get wiped out.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:31.300
By 2021, Bill’s personal net worth stood
at $20 billion, but he was trading with $100

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:35.680
billion meaning that he had $80 billion worth
of debt spread across multiple banks.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.529
This means that if Bill’s positions dipped
just 20%, he would have a negative net worth

00:10:39.529 --> 00:10:41.209
and he should be margin called.

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:42.779
But, banks didn’t know this.

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:48.019
They thought they had 40 or 50% worth of leeway
and this became painfully evident when one

00:10:48.019 --> 00:10:50.199
of Bill’s biggest positions crumbled.

00:10:50.199 --> 00:10:54.240
In March of 2021, ViacomCBS announced that
they would do a share offering which caused

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.610
the stock to tumble over 50% within a week.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:02.290
Given that this was one of Archegos’ biggest
positions, banks started to margin call Bill

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.230
and this is when they realized they were screwed.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:09.480
Not only had Bill blown away his entire net
worth, but he had also caused banks including

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:14.699
Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, Credit Suisse,
and Nomura to incur $10 billion worth of losses.

00:11:14.699 --> 00:11:17.800
And with that Archegos filed for bankruptcy.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:22.750
THE AFTERMATH:
At the end of the day, no one really cared

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.649
about the banks losing money as it was due
to their own lack of due diligence.

00:11:26.649 --> 00:11:29.440
But, many were sad that Bill lost all his
money.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.449
You see, while Bill definitely had a gambling
problem, he was putting his winnings to good

00:11:34.449 --> 00:11:35.449
use.

00:11:35.449 --> 00:11:37.570
He wasn’t blowing it on yachts and jets.

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:43.250
Instead, he lived an extremely modest life
and donated a total of $590 million to churches,

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:44.459
schools, and charities.

00:11:44.459 --> 00:11:49.030
And Bill has repeatedly iterated that his
greatest love in life is god and that the

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:52.160
primary reason he was chasing money was so
that he could donate it.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.639
So, it’s really quite a shame that things
have to come to this, but he should’ve thought

00:11:56.639 --> 00:12:00.330
about that before he was gambled with $100
billion.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.069
But that’s just what I think.

00:12:02.069 --> 00:12:06.279
Do you guys think Bill has had a net positive
or net negative impact on the world?

00:12:06.279 --> 00:12:07.410
Comment that down below.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.769
Also, drop a like if you respect Bill’s
balls of steel.

00:12:10.769 --> 00:12:14.399
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in different

00:12:14.399 --> 00:12:26.800
languages and consider subscribing to see
more questions logically answered.

